EXT. VILLAGE PIER, TUG-OF-WAR - SUNSET

A rope as thick as a man's wrist lies across the weathered planks of a fishing pier, its centre marked with a red rag. Below, the tide slaps the pilings. A crowd of villagers presses along the railings, quiet.

SAOIRSE KEANE, forty, anchor of the harbour team, wraps the rope twice round her forearm and plants her boots against a plank she has used for twenty years. Opposite her, FINBARR MCGRATH, her cousin and rival, spits on his palms and rubs them together, grinning like a man who has already won. Behind him the rest of his team braces. At the rail, old DONAL, the referee, lifts a handkerchief, and beside him NUALA, thirteen, Saoirse's daughter, grips the rail so hard her knuckles whiten.

DONAL
Take the strain!

Saoirse leans back. Finbarr leans back. The red rag does not move. A gull settles on a piling to watch.

FINBARR
Still got your mother's hands, Saoirse.

SAOIRSE
And you've still got her mouth.

The rope groans. A plank cracks under Finbarr's heel and the rag slides a foot toward the harbour side. Nuala shrieks. Saoirse digs in, the cords of her neck standing out, her boots skating on salt-slick wood.

NUALA
Mam, don't let go!

FINBARR
Let go, Saoirse! Let go before you're hurt!

SAOIRSE
(through her teeth)
Not this year, Fin.

The rag crosses the line. Finbarr's team stumbles. Then the old rope, frayed through twenty summers, parts with a gunshot crack, and both teams go down in a tangle of limbs and oilskin, Saoirse across her cousin's chest, and the pier erupts.

DONAL
No contest!

NUALA
(laughing, crying)
Mam!
